{
  "image_name": "9cf994f20dfcdbffc0d0eb6ffa6b18fc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares paraelectric and ferroelectric dielectric behavior and shows how this affects capacitance and possible negative-capacitance operation. Red denotes a paraelectric response, while blue denotes a ferroelectric one.\n\nAcross the top panels, the paraelectric material has a smooth, monotonic relation between polarization/charge and electric field/voltage, whereas the ferroelectric material shows an S-shaped, nonmonotonic response with unstable middle regions and switching between two stable branches. The corresponding capacitance plots emphasize this contrast: the paraelectric capacitance peaks smoothly near zero field, while the ferroelectric case splits into two branches and can pass through a regime where the inverse capacitance becomes negative. In the energy-like plot, the paraelectric curve appears as a single well, but the ferroelectric curve is a double well, indicating two stable polarization states.\n\nThe bottom part illustrates what happens when a ferroelectric is combined with a linear dielectric. For a “dielectrically soft” system with large series capacitance, the operating line intersects the ferroelectric curve in the usual hysteretic way, giving regular ferroelectric switching. For a “dielectrically stiff” system with small series capacitance, the line can stabilize the otherwise unstable central part of the ferroelectric curve, consistent with incipient negative capacitance behavior.",
